skill: Data Analysis Fundamentals

@ Data Cleaning | B
Which of the following is a data cleaning task?
= Removing duplicate records and fixing inconsistent formats
- Adding random noise to every value
- Copying rows to make the data set bigger
- Encrypting the data set
? Cleaning improves quality by removing duplicates, correcting errors and standardizing values.

@ Data Cleaning | B
What is an outlier?
= A value that differs markedly from the other observations
- The most common value in a data set
- A missing value
- The average of the data
? Outliers may be errors or genuine rare events, so they should be investigated before being removed.

@ Data Cleaning | I
About 5 percent of the values in a numeric column are missing at random. Which approach is commonly reasonable?
= Impute the values, for example with the median, or drop the affected rows depending on context
- Fill every gap with the text "unknown"
- Delete the whole column without checking
- Ignore the gaps and continue silently
? The right treatment depends on how much data is missing and why. The choice should be documented.

@ Data Cleaning | I
A data set stores dates as 01/02/2025, 2025-02-01 and Feb 1 2025. What is the best cleaning step?
= Convert all dates to one consistent format
- Delete the date column
- Keep the mixed formats so no information is lost
- Convert dates to random numbers
? Consistent formats are needed for sorting, filtering and grouping by date.

@ Data Cleaning | I
Why should you check for duplicate records before calculating totals?
= Duplicates can inflate counts and sums
- Duplicates reduce the file size
- Duplicates always improve accuracy
- Totals ignore duplicates automatically
? Counting the same transaction twice overstates the result.

@ Data Cleaning | A
Which rule is commonly used to flag outliers with quartiles?
= Values more than 1.5 times the IQR below Q1 or above Q3
- Values equal to the median
- Values that appear more than once
- Values that are exactly zero
? The interquartile range rule is robust because it does not depend on the mean.

@ Data Visualization | B
Which chart type best displays how monthly revenue changed across a year?
= Line chart
- Pie chart
- Stacked radar chart
- Word cloud
? Line charts show change over an ordered sequence such as time.

@ Data Visualization | B
Which chart is best for comparing the sales of five product categories?
= Bar or column chart
- Line chart with one point
- Scatter plot of one variable
- Gauge chart
? Bars are easy to compare because length is judged accurately.

@ Data Visualization | I
What is a scatter plot mainly used to show?
= The relationship between two numeric variables
- The share of each category in a total
- A single number against a target
- The order of steps in a process
? Each point shows two values, so patterns and correlations become visible.

@ Data Visualization | I
What is a common problem with 3D pie charts?
= They distort proportions and make comparison difficult
- They cannot show percentages
- They need no data
- They always show trends over time
? Perspective makes some slices look bigger than they are.

@ Data Visualization | I
Which chart shows the distribution of a single numeric variable?
= Histogram
- Pie chart
- Line chart
- Treemap
? A histogram groups values into bins and shows how many fall in each.

@ Data Interpretation | I
Sales were 200 in Q1 and 250 in Q2. What is the percentage growth from Q1 to Q2?
= 25%
- 20%
- 50%
- 12.5%
? Growth = (250 - 200) / 200 = 0.25, which is 25%.

@ Data Interpretation | I
A chart shows that 40% of 500 surveyed customers prefer Product A. How many customers is that?
= 200
- 40
- 250
- 125
? 40% of 500 = 0.4 x 500 = 200.

@ Data Interpretation | A
The average of five numbers is 20. After a sixth number is added the average becomes 22. What is the sixth number?
= 32
- 22
- 42
- 26
? The new total is 6 x 22 = 132. The old total was 5 x 20 = 100, so the new number is 32.

@ Data Interpretation | A
Revenue increases by 20% and then decreases by 20%. Compared with the original value the final revenue is:
= 4% lower
- Unchanged
- 4% higher
- 20% lower
? 1.20 x 0.80 = 0.96 of the original, which is 4% lower.

@ Analytical Reasoning | I
A website had 2,000 visits and 50 orders. What is the conversion rate (orders divided by visits)?
= 2.5%
- 4%
- 25%
- 0.25%
? 50 / 2000 = 0.025, which is 2.5%.

@ Analytical Reasoning | A
A metric rose after a marketing campaign. What is needed to claim the campaign caused the increase?
= A controlled comparison such as an A/B test or a control group
- Only the fact that the metric rose
- A larger chart
- A higher number of dashboards
? Without a comparison group, other factors such as seasonality could explain the change.

@ Analytical Reasoning | I
You receive a vague business question from a manager. What is the best first step?
= Clarify the objective and agree on how success will be measured
- Start building charts immediately
- Collect every data set available
- Wait for the manager to find the answer
? A clear question and metric prevent wasted analysis and wrong conclusions.
